#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""macpulse — a Mac health scanner for the AI-development era.

Every Mac cleaner on the market measures the same thing: disk space. Caches, logs,
language files, "junk". That model was written for a Mac whose problem was a full
disk, and it is blind to what actually brings a modern developer's Mac to its knees:

  * a 300 KB process pinned at 145% CPU for six days, from an app that was deleted
    (invisible to every size-based scan, because it is small)
  * an AI coding assistant where each open session spawns its own full MCP server
    stack, so ten tabs quietly cost 3-5 GB of duplicated infrastructure
  * an MCP server that has been failing authentication for weeks but still starts
    three node processes every single time
  * a browser that will not launch because a lock file points at a PID that died
  * headless Chrome instances left behind by browser automation, running for days

None of that is junk on a disk. All of it is live waste, and it is what a machine
running Claude Code, Cursor, Docker and a dozen MCP servers actually suffers from.
macpulse looks for exactly these, ranks them by what they cost you, and can fix them.

Read-only by default. Nothing is killed or deleted without --fix.

Usage:
  macpulse                 scan and print a health report
  macpulse --ui            open the app window (local, loopback only)
  macpulse --report        also write an HTML report and open it
  macpulse --fix           scan, then offer each fix one by one
  macpulse --fix --yes     apply every safe fix without asking
  macpulse --json          machine-readable output
  macpulse --brief         one line per warning, silent when healthy
  macpulse --lang az|en|ru force a language (default: follow the system)
  macpulse --exit-status   exit 0 healthy, 1 warning, 2 critical, 3 partly blind scan
  macpulse --metrics       Prometheus/OpenMetrics text (score, swap, pressure, findings)
  macpulse --doctor        check macpulse's own installation (add --json for machine output)
  macpulse --mcp           serve a read-only MCP server over stdio (scan, explain_score)
"""

